gui.autocal.doc.line.001=and inconsistencies.
gui.autocal.doc.line.002=leaving the computer turned on.
gui.autocal.doc.line.003=May cause unintended behavior
gui.autocal.doc.line.004=Skips instructions that error,
gui.autocal.doc.line.005=or after finishing.
gui.autocal.doc.line.006=Restarts the computer automatically when
gui.autocal.doc.line.007=the program stops due to an error
gui.autocal.doc.line.008=The sixth button is for downloading a script file. The existing `script.txt` is deleted and replaced with the script that is loaded onto the AUTOCAL unit.
gui.autocal.doc.line.009=Read this document carefully as all the described concepts are vital for using the AUTOCAL unit.
gui.autocal.doc.line.010=The script read by AUTOCAL units is written in *Machine Script - Equestrian Standard, Version 1*, or MS-ES1. MS-ES1 features named variables, value comparison, (conditional) jumping, evaluation of mathematical expressions and reading/writing of RoR signals. The speed at which lines are processed is defined by the *clock speed*, which can be defined in the script. The amount describes the number of lines processed per tick, i.e. the default clock speed of 1 means 20 lines are processed per second (there are 20 ticks in one second). The maximum clock speed is determined by the server config value `AUTOCAL_MAX_CLOCK` (default is 20, i.e. 400 per second).
gui.autocal.doc.line.011=MS-ES v1.1 (or MS-ES1 FEID, "First Extended Instruction Set") is the second released version of the AUTOCAL's script interpreter. It features various useful additions such as text splitting, substrings, a stack-based buffer and world time grabbing. The stack for example allows for a call stack, which means that functions that were jumped to can now be nested without having to save the most recent jumping points in dedicated variables. The string split and substring operations now allow for reversing concat operations and potentially grabbing individual coordinates out of combined coordinate strings. A single RoR send operation can now send serialized data (i.e. multiple values in a single text string) which can now finally be de-serialized again.
gui.autocal.doc.line.012=Programs witten in MS-ES1 are largely compatible with MS-ES1.1 interpreters, however, MS-ES1.1 now imposes a character limit of 256 on buffered values.
gui.autocal.doc.line.013=The buffer is a single "slot" of information that can be used for many commands. Some commands produce an output which is saved to the buffer, some commands modify the contents of the buffer, and some commands use the buffer's contents. The buffer's contents can also be saved permanently as a named variable, and named variables can be written back into the buffer again if needed. The buffer persists as long as the AUTOCAL unit is running, should it restart or shut down, the buffer's contents are lost.
gui.autocal.doc.line.014=The stack functions similar to the buffer, as in there is only one single stack that can be accessed per AUTOCAL unit, and it can save arbitrary text data. The difference being, while the buffer can only store one value, the stack can store a list of up to 256 values. Values on a stack follow the last-in-first-out principle, i.e. the most recently added values are read first. The stack supports `push` (adding new elements), `pop` (reading and removing elements) and `peek` (reading but not removing elements).
gui.autocal.doc.line.015=MS-ES1 allows named variables to be saved for later use. There is no limit to how many variables can be saved. All variables are text ("Strings"), however depending on the command and context, that text may be interpreted as a number (both full and decimal). Variables, much like the buffer, are also stored as long as the program is running, if the AUTOCAL unit shuts down, all stored variables are lost.
gui.autocal.doc.line.016=Many commands allow for *variable substitution*, i.e. a specific format can be used to insert the contents of a variable (or multiple!) into a parameter. This allows for the quick use of variables, or multiple variables in the same statement. Substitution is defined by `$variable name$`, where this text is replaced with the value of a variable called "variable name".
gui.autocal.doc.line.017=Special case: The contents of the buffer can also be accessed using substitution using `$buffer$`. Consequently, a variable also named "buffer" can **not** be accessed using variable substitution at all.
gui.autocal.doc.line.018=RoR has a specific limitation: A signal cannot be sent on the same channel within the same game tick. Subsequent signals on the same channel in the same tick will overwrite the previous one, with the exception of numeric (whole number) signals which are added together (e.g. sending "5" and "7" in the same tick creates a signal "12"). Since the AUTOCAL unit's clock speed allows it to theoretically send multiple signals on the same channel on the same tick, it might be necessary to *end* the operation for this tick even though there's still clock cycles left to do. This can be done with the `endtick` command.
gui.autocal.doc.line.019=Example: `/ntmserver set AUTOCAL_MAX_CLOCK 10` -> Sets the max clock speed to 10.
gui.autocal.doc.line.020=Example: `# This is a comment` -> A line that does nothing, but can still be useful to explain and annotate other commands.
gui.autocal.doc.line.021=`nop` (no operation) is an operation that consumes one clock cycle, but does not have any other effect. This is only useful in special cases where clock cycle timing is somehow important.
gui.autocal.doc.line.022=`clockspeed <speed>` sets the AUTOCAL's clock speed (i.e. amount of lines processed per tick). This can be changed at any point in the script, but usually it is most useful to start the script by defining the clock speed.
gui.autocal.doc.line.023=Example: `clockspeed 5` -> Sets the AUTOCAL's clock speed to five lines per tick (100 lines per second).
gui.autocal.doc.line.024=`dest <name>` creates a jump destination. Using the various jumping conditions, we can cause the AUTOCAL to return back (or forward) to this point. If a destination is reached not by jumping but simply by reading the next line, it will not use up a clock cycle, just like a comment. Destinations need unique names, if multiple destinations exist with the same name, then the latter ones will overwrite the former ones.
gui.autocal.doc.line.025=Example: `dest start` -> Creates a destination point named "start", any jump instruction using "start" will cause the script to return to this point.
gui.autocal.doc.line.026=Example: `eval $val1$ + $val2$` assuming `val1` is 4 and `val2` is 8 would resolve to `eval 4 + 8`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.027=Example: `jmp $dest$` -> Jumps to the destination point with the same name as the contents of the variable "dest".
gui.autocal.doc.line.028=`jmpif <destination>` will cause the program to skip to the destination with the supplied name, if the buffer's content is `true`. Otherwise, the program will just proceed to the next line, like any other instruction. Supports variable substitution!
gui.autocal.doc.line.029=-> If the buffer is `true`, then the program will jump to the destination point named "skip" and only run the second `nop`. Otherwise, both the first and second `nop` will run.
gui.autocal.doc.line.030=`jmpnot <destination>` will cause the program to skip to the destination with the supplied name, if the buffer's content is **not** `true`. This is basically the inverse of `jmpif`. Only exists for some convenience for people who are used to more traditional languages' `if` statements. Supports variable substitution!
gui.autocal.doc.line.031=-> By using two jumps, one conditional and one fixed, we can emulate the structure of an `if/else` block. If the buffer's content is `true`, the first `nop` runs. Otherwise, the second `nop` is run. The second jump (`jmp end`) lets us skip over the "else" part of the script after running the "then" part.
gui.autocal.doc.line.032=`endtick` stops the script until the next game tick. This is important when sending multiple RoR signals on the same channel in a row, since a channel in the same tick can only hold one signal.
gui.autocal.doc.line.033=`shutdown` will turn the AUTOCAL unit off. If the AUTOCAL is set up to automatically reboot, this effectively restarts the script from scratch, voiding all saved variables and the buffer. If not, then the AUTOCAL unit will stay off until manually restarted.
gui.autocal.doc.line.034=`load <name>` will take the value of a variable with the supplied name and copy it to the buffer. Many commands work directly out of the buffer, so next to variable substitution, this is the only way of actually accessing the contents of a variable.
gui.autocal.doc.line.035=Example: `load val` -> Assuming that the variable "val" contains the value `5`, then the buffer's value is now overwritten with that `5`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.036=`save <name>` will take the value of the buffer and save it to a variable with the supplied name. This is the only way of actually changing variables, and saving values for later use besides the buffer.
gui.autocal.doc.line.037=Example: `jmp start` -> Jumps to the destination point named "start".
gui.autocal.doc.line.038=`buffer <value>` will write the supplied value directly to the buffer. This means that commands that require buffer values can be supplied with values directly from the code.
gui.autocal.doc.line.039=`push [value]` will add the supplied value to the stack. If no value is supplied, it will write the buffer's contents to the stack instead. Do note that the stack can only hold up to 256 values! Supports variable substitution!
gui.autocal.doc.line.040=Example:
gui.autocal.doc.line.041=`jmpif skip`
gui.autocal.doc.line.042=`nop`
gui.autocal.doc.line.043=`dest skip`
gui.autocal.doc.line.044=`evalr [statement]` is identical to `eval`, however it will round the result to the nearest whole number. Whole numbers are important for RoR, since gauges, numeric displays and logic receivers can only handle whole numbers, and not decimals.
gui.autocal.doc.line.045=`rounddown` or `floor` will try to interpret the buffer's content as a decimal, and round it **down** to the next lower integer, writing the result to the buffer again.
gui.autocal.doc.line.046=`jmpnot skip` <- if
gui.autocal.doc.line.047=`nop`         <- then
gui.autocal.doc.line.048=`jmp end`
gui.autocal.doc.line.049=`nop`         <- else
gui.autocal.doc.line.050=`dest end`    <- end
gui.autocal.doc.line.051=Example: `eq Brick` -> If the buffer is `Brick`, then it is set to `true`, otherwise it becomes `false`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.052=Example: `eq $comp$` -> If the buffer's value is equal to the value of the variable "comp", then it is set to `true`, otherwise it becomes `false`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.053=`gtb <value>` - greater than buffer, will try to compare the buffer to the supplied value. The buffer will be set to `true` if the supplied numerical value is greater and `false` otherwise. Supports variable substitution!
gui.autocal.doc.line.054=Example: `gtb 4` -> If the buffer is `3` or lower, then it is set to `true`, otherwise it becomes `false`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.055=Example: `gtb $comp$` -> If the buffer lower than the value of "comp", then it is set to `true`, otherwise it becomes `false`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.056=`ltb <value>` - less than buffer, will try to compare the buffer to the supplied value. The buffer will be set to `true` if the supplied numerical value is lower and `false` otherwise. Supports variable substitution!
gui.autocal.doc.line.057=Example: `ltb 4` -> If the buffer is `5` or higher, then it is set to `true`, otherwise it becomes `false`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.058=Example: `ltb $comp$` -> If the buffer higher than the value of "comp", then it is set to `true`, otherwise it becomes `false`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.059=`buffer Horseshoe`
gui.autocal.doc.line.060=`save item`
gui.autocal.doc.line.061=-> Will buffer the value `Horseshoe` and then save it to the variable called "item".
gui.autocal.doc.line.062=`length` will count the number of characters in the buffer's stored text, and write that value to the buffer.
gui.autocal.doc.line.063=`pop` will remove the most recently added value from the stack and write it to the buffer.
gui.autocal.doc.line.064=`last <amount>` will grab the last x characters from the buffer and write them to the buffer. This is a substring operation that always ends at the last letter.
gui.autocal.doc.line.065=`send <channel>` will send a Redstone-over-Radio signal over the supplied channel, with the signal's value being the current buffer's value. Sending repeatedly over the same channel requires waiting for a full game tick, so using `endtick` after sending is advised. Supports variable substitution!
gui.autocal.doc.line.066=Example: `eval 4 + 5` -> Calculates "4+5" and saves `9` to the buffer.
gui.autocal.doc.line.067=-> Will take the signal from the RoR channel "input", multiply it by 100, and send that value on the channel "output".
gui.autocal.doc.line.068=`load calc`
gui.autocal.doc.line.069=`eval`
gui.autocal.doc.line.070=Basic if/else conditions are the bread and butter of most programming. If we want to change the behavior based on different values, we need to compare, and then conditional jump.
gui.autocal.doc.line.071=This example creates a script that processed a number "val" based on how high it is. 4 and below are multiplied by 2, otherwise it is divided by 2:
gui.autocal.doc.line.072=People who are used to high languages will already know this concept, reusable parts of code with a set of parameters and an optional return value. For this we will use a few variables and a set of jumps in order to be able to use this piece of code from anywhere:
gui.autocal.doc.line.073=Example: `rounddown` -> Assuming the buffer's value is `4.2`, the buffer's new value will be `4`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.074=Example: `rounddown` -> Assuming the buffer's value is `4.6`, the buffer's new value will be `4`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.076=Example: `roundup` -> Assuming the buffer's value is `4.2`, the buffer's new value will be `5`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.077=Example: `roundup` -> Assuming the buffer's value is `4.6`, the buffer's new value will be `5`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.079=Example: `round` -> Assuming the buffer's value is `4.2`, the buffer's new value will be `4`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.080=Example: `round` -> Assuming the buffer's value is `4.6`, the buffer's new value will be `5`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.092=`geb <value>` - greater than or equal buffer.
gui.autocal.doc.line.093=`leb <value>` - less than or equal buffer.
gui.autocal.doc.line.096=`buffer cats;and;dogs`
gui.autocal.doc.line.097=`split 3`
gui.autocal.doc.line.098=-> Will buffer the value of the third fragment, being `dogs`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.100=`buffer cats;and;dogs;and;birds`
gui.autocal.doc.line.101=`splitcount`
gui.autocal.doc.line.102=-> Will buffer the number of fragments, being `5`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.104=`buffer cats and dogs`
gui.autocal.doc.line.105=`lenght`
gui.autocal.doc.line.106=-> Will buffer the number of characters in `cats and dogs`, being `13`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.108=`first 4`
gui.autocal.doc.line.109=-> Will buffer the first four characters of the buffer, `cats`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.111=`last 4`
gui.autocal.doc.line.112=-> Will buffer the last four characters of the buffer, `dogs`.
gui.autocal.doc.line.114=`buffer Hello!`
gui.autocal.doc.line.115=`send transmission`
gui.autocal.doc.line.116=-> Will send the RoR signal `Hello!` on the channel "transmission".
gui.autocal.doc.line.117=`buffer SOS`
gui.autocal.doc.line.118=`send $target$`
gui.autocal.doc.line.119=-> Will send the RoR signal "SOS" to the channel saved in the variable "target".
gui.autocal.doc.line.121=`listen input`
gui.autocal.doc.line.122=`eval $buffer$ * 100`
gui.autocal.doc.line.123=`send output`
gui.autocal.doc.line.129=`buffer 4`       <- buffer `4` for comparison
gui.autocal.doc.line.130=`gtb $val$`      <- is "val" greater than our buffer?
gui.autocal.doc.line.131=`jmpnot else`    <- if not, jump to "else"
gui.autocal.doc.line.132=`eval $val$ / 2` <- if it is, divide by 2
gui.autocal.doc.line.133=`save val`       <- ...and save to "val"
gui.autocal.doc.line.134=`jmp end`        <- now jump to "end" to skip our "else" block
gui.autocal.doc.line.135=`dest else`      <- else...
gui.autocal.doc.line.136=`eval $val$ * 2` <- multiply by 2
gui.autocal.doc.line.137=`save val`       <- ...and save to val
gui.autocal.doc.line.138=`dest end`       <- no matter which branch we took, we always end up here
gui.autocal.doc.line.140=This example implements a basic lerp (linear interpolation) function.
gui.autocal.doc.line.141=`dest lerp`
gui.autocal.doc.line.142=`eval $a$ + ($b$ - $a$) * $i$`
gui.autocal.doc.line.143=`save result`
gui.autocal.doc.line.144=`jmp $return$`
gui.autocal.doc.line.146=`buffer 4`          <- first we set up our parameters to be used in the function
gui.autocal.doc.line.147=`save a`
gui.autocal.doc.line.148=`buffer 7`
gui.autocal.doc.line.149=`save b`
gui.autocal.doc.line.150=`buffer 0.6`
gui.autocal.doc.line.151=`save i`
gui.autocal.doc.line.152=`buffer returnhere` <- then we define the name of the return point
gui.autocal.doc.line.153=`save return`
gui.autocal.doc.line.154=`jmp lerp`          <- call the function
gui.autocal.doc.line.155=`dest returnhere`   <- once the function concludes, we are back here
gui.autocal.doc.line.156=At the end of it all, the variable "result" now has the desired value.
